Every function parameter has a name and an explicit type.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    describe |prefix String, subject String| -> String:
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    ;
]

### Parameter forms

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name Type
    name ~Type
    name Type = default
]

- `name Type` declares a shared parameter.
- `name ~Type` declares a mutable or exclusive parameter.
- `= default` provides a value when the caller omits that slot.
- Parameter names follow the no-shadowing rule.
- Each parameter name must be unique in the signature.

`$` is not a reactive parameter mode. Any future reactive parameter spelling remains
owned by its accepted Wiring design rather than this function-parameter contract.

Directive calls share the ordinary argument parser and slot routing. Ordinary `f()`
remains valid. Only directive syntax rejects an authored empty `()`.

### Defaults

A default belongs to the declaration.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    describe |prefix String = "item", subject String| -> String:
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    ;
]

A caller can omit a defaulted parameter.

Named arguments can skip an earlier default:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    label = describe(subject = "apple")
]

A positional call cannot skip a slot in the middle. Use a named argument when
the call needs to reach a later parameter.

### Default value constraints

A parameter default must be a compile-time value.

Accepted:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    DEFAULT_PREFIX #= "item"

    describe |prefix String = DEFAULT_PREFIX, subject String| -> String:
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    ;
]

Rejected:

- runtime bindings
- runtime function calls
- references to other parameters

Struct fields share the same `= default` syntax, but struct defaults have their
own compile-time rule. Choice payload fields do not support defaults.

Pipes declare parameters or typed fields. Supplied values use
@../language-overview/#mon-syntax (MON syntax). An anonymous const record requires
an initializer for every field and declares no constructable type. Its entries
are values, not parameters or defaults. See @../constants/#const-records (Const records).

Declaration lists remain flat. Nesting a legal value expression in a default does
not introduce a nested type declaration or make an anonymous record compatible
with a named parameter type.

### MON data boundary

Source parameter defaults above are unchanged. MON schema defaults are a separate serialised-data contract documented by reference: defaults are prevalidated data applied only at the exact missing field, a supplied `none` is validated as `none` and never replaced, and a supplied nested record is never deep-merged with a default. Only an explicit field default permits omission, and choice payload fields still have no defaults. That contract is Rust-only serialised-data documentation. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format) and @../errors/#options (Options).
